  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe adopt a hybrid approach inspired by simulated annealing and the gradient-based refinement of the previous program. The goal is to transform the high-dimensional non-convex optimization problem into a sequence of local searches over a distributed structure. We use a multi-resolution pyramid encoding where each resolution level corresponds to a different decomposition of $[0,2]$. The key novelty is the introduction of a spherical gradient descent for each pyramid node, ensuring smooth boundary interpolation while allowing global exploration through flipping operations.\n\nThe algorithm first generates several pyramid configurations that approximate the solution space broadly. Each pyramid node represents a $(n-1)$-dimensional simplex that projects onto neighboring nodes. This allows us to navigate through local optimality conditions (gradients) while maintaining global resolution through flipping operations across scales. By starting with large-step simplex flips in coarse pyramids and refining to dense local searches, we escape local optima while efficiently exploring the search space.\n\nThe repair mechanism ensures integral constraint satisfaction by distributing any integral deviation uniformly across the reconstructed vectors, using a $\\ell_q$-like distribution with $q=1.0$ to maintain unitarity.\n\nWe address the non-convex nature of the problem by leveraging local searches and copious star references to the entire pyramid structure. The global step is designed to escape local optima, while the local step ensures fine-grained optimization along each direction.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Variables: We employ a pyramid structure of size $n$, defined by base $b$ and height levels $k=0,...,m$, yielding nodes $P_j$ for $j=0,...,n_b$\n- Objective: $\\min C_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k))\\,dx$\n- Constraints: $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$ and $\\sum_{i=1}^n h_i = n/2$\n- Decomposition: For any $P_j$ at any layer, we can write its value at a point $x$ as a linear combination of neighbors in adjacent layers: $P_j(x) = S_j(x) \\cdot \\ell_j(x) + \\epsilon$\n- Flips: We implement two flip operations: layer-flipping (introduces curvature) and pyramid-flipping (changes resolution)\n- Repair: $h' = \\text{clip}(h + \\text{repair}_{\\text{proj}, h) \\rightarrow \\text{satisfy constraint entirely}$\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n**Preprocessor:**\n- Constructs pyramidal layers from (possibly new) base config\n- Reconstructs input pyramid from parent sample if available\n- Normalizes and wraps signal processing parameters to $[0,2]$\n\n**Pyramid Initial Constructor (create_pyramid):**\n1. Input: $n$, target integral $n/2$\n2. Initialize $h$ with zeros at all layers\n3. Generate base $p_0 \\sim \\text{Uniform}[0,1]^{n_0}$\n4. For each layer $k$ from 1 to $m$:\n   - $p_k = 1 - \\text{smoothstep}(p_{k-1}, \\alpha_k)$\n   - $p_k = \\text{proj}_{\\text{base}}(p_k)$\n5. Return $(p_1,...,p_m)$\n\n**Pyramid Repair (repair_pyramid):**\n1. Compute total integral $I = \\sum_{i} h_i$\n2. If $|I - n/2| \\geq \\epsilon_0$, distribute surplus/deficit uniformly with:\n   - $h = h \\odot \\text{scale}(I, n/2)$\n   - Clip values at $[0,1]$\n\n**Spherical Local Descent (gradient_step):**\n1. Input: current $h$, descent $\\eta$\n2. Compute gradient $g = \\nabla \\text{Loss}(h)$\n3. Project to simplex: $v = \\text{proj}(g)$\n4. Take step: $h' = h + \\eta \\cdot v$\n5. Clip and repair: $h'' = \\text{repair}(h')$\n\n**Global Flip (layer_flip):**\n1. Choose random layer $k$\n2. For each node in layer $k$, flip signal with strength $\\epsilon_k$\n   - $p_i' = p_i + \\epsilon_k \\cdot rand()\\text{ sign}( \\beta_i )$\n\n**Main Loop:**\n1. $t_0 = start_time; t_max = budget_s$\n2. $h = \\text{preinit()}\\text{ from} initial\\_h\\_values$\n3. $pyramid = \\text{create\\_with\\_pyramid}(h)$\n4. Initialize best $ = current$\n5. Initialize best_score from evaluation\n6. For iteration in range(time-based):\n   - With prob $p_g$: Perform global(pyramid) search (30%, depth 3)\n   - With prob $1-p_g$: Perform gradient descent (70%, epoch 5)\n   - After every 10 iterations: Apply integral repair if needed\n   - Time check: if $current < best\\_score$: accept move\n   - Record best solution ever found\n7. If time: return final solution with evaluation\n\n4. Search Choices\n- Base pyramid size $n=200-1000$ (adaptive)\n- Flip strength $\\epsilon_k = 0.1 / level^2$\n- Gradient step size $\\eta = 1e-3$ (Spherical)\n- Repair depth $v=2$ minimum: checks for integral satisfaction\n- Acceptance probability $p_g=0.3$\n- $p$ uniform over simplex: $1e-2$\n\n5. Budgeting\n- $50\\%$ for global exploration\n- $50\\%$ for local refinement\n- Warm-restarts every 300s to $90\\%$ remaining budget\n- Abort if no improvement in 20 consecutive iterations\n\n6. Validation\n1. Check dimension post-initialization: len(parent) must match $n$\n2. Post- repair:verify constraints (0<=x<=1 and sum==n/2.0)\n3. Check finite values in correlation computation\n4. final output: tuple(h_values,  ${best\\_score}$, n_points)\n\nThis approach leverages multi-scale structure and renormalization to maintain global perspectives while fine refining locally, explicitly targeting the boundaries of the previous solution's local minimum.",
